# PostgreSQL Rules

## Types (use these, not the tempting alternatives)
| Use case | Correct | Avoid |
|---|---|---|
| IDs | `bigint` / `uuid` | `int`, random uuid in hot path |
| Strings | `text` | `varchar(n)` |
| Timestamps | `timestamptz` | `timestamp` |
| Money | `numeric(12,2)` | `float` |
| JSON | `jsonb` | `json` |

## Indexes
- B-tree default; composite `(a, b)` with equality columns first.
- `GIN` for `jsonb @>` and full-text.
- `BRIN` for wide time-series ranges.
- Partial indexes for soft-delete: `WHERE deleted_at IS NULL`.

## Queries
- Parameterized only — never string interpolation.
- No `SELECT *` in services. No N+1 loops — batch with `IN (...)` or JOIN.
- Every hot query has an `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` showing index use.

## Migrations
- One concern per migration; reversible where possible.
- Non-null on big table: add nullable → backfill in batches → add NOT NULL.
- Drops are two-phase (stop reading → deploy → drop).

## RLS (Supabase / multi-tenant)
- ON for every user-data table. Default deny.
- Policies per role per action. Tested with anon + authed roles in CI.
- Never rely on client-side tenant filtering.
